   "emerged": "late 90s",
   "aka": "Minimal House, Glitch House",
   "paras": [
    {
     "en": "You know how sometimes you download (and by download I mean purchase legitimately from an online music store) a totally legal mp3 (from a completely authorized source that sells music downloads approved by the artists and the recording industry in a legit and secure manner and is not involved in piracy at all), but the music has little hiccups and twerping noises in it?",
     "zh": "你知道那种情况吧：你下载（我说的「下载」是指从在线音乐商店合法购买）一首完全合法的 mp3（来自完全授权、经艺人和唱片业批准、以合法安全方式销售音乐下载、与盗版毫无关系的来源），结果音乐里有细小的爆音和「啾啾」声？"
    },
    {
     "en": "Those are called artifacts -- little audio errors that occurred in the mp3 digital encoding process. They're the audio equivalent of compression artifacts in low quality Photoshopped images.",
     "zh": "那些东西叫 artifacts（伪影）——mp3 数字编码过程中产生的微小音频错误。它们相当于低质量 PS 图片上的压缩伪影的音频版。"
    },
    {
     "en": "Artifacts were quite common back in the 90s when mp3s (that everyone purchased legally from licensed sources approved by the RIAA) proliferated as low quality 128 kbps audio files because file size was an issue back then.",
     "zh": "90 年代 artifacts 相当常见，那时 mp3（人人都从 RIAA 批准的授权来源合法购买）以 128 kbps 的低质量音频格式泛滥，因为当年文件大小是个大问题。"
    },
    {
     "en": "Sometimes it was difficult to find a good mp3 of a song (that you are allowed to own a digital copy of because you have the original CD) that didn't have any artifacts. Nobody liked artifacts and it was a rough period where procuring quality music (through completely valid and legitimate channels that you totally pay for honestly) was difficult. Such are the perils of (purchasing completely legal) music (via honest means that you are allowed to own and do not steal, copy or pirate in any way) back in the day.",
     "zh": "有时候很难找到一首没有 artifacts 的好 mp3（你本来就有权拥有它的数字副本，因为你买了原版 CD）。没人喜欢 artifacts，那是一段艰难的时期——想弄到高质量音乐（通过完全正当合法的渠道，你确实老老实实付了钱）非常困难。这就是当年（通过诚实手段、你被允许拥有、绝不偷窃复制或盗版）购买音乐的种种艰险。"
    },
    {
     "en": "Of course that didn't stop some dumbass from thinking that maybe mp3 artifacts can be harnessed into a new style of music the same way a creative person looks at garbage and tries to make it art. And that's why we have Microhouse.",
     "zh": "当然，这没能阻止某个蠢货想：也许 mp3 artifacts 可以被驯化成一种新的音乐风格，就像有创造力的人看着垃圾试图把它变成艺术一样。于是我们有了 Microhouse。"
    },
    {
     "en": "It's like an auto company coming up with a new type of car with a special engine that rattles all the time, the steering wheel shudders at high speed, and the muffler is hanging low and dragging on the ground. Those aren't bugs, they're features!",
     "zh": "这就像一家汽车公司推出了一款新车，装了个一直咔嗒响的特制引擎，方向盘在高速时会发抖，排气管垂得低低的在地上拖。这些不是缺陷，是特性！"
    },
    {
     "en": "A lot of genres are based on mistakes (happy accidents according to one B. Ross). Microhouse is the only genre based on an actual error.",
     "zh": "很多流派都建立在错误之上（按 B. Ross 的说法叫「快乐的小意外」）。Microhouse 是唯一一个建立在真正的错误之上的流派。"
    },
    {
     "en": "Before long this gave producers the idea to \"glitch\" up their productions with slices of samples instead of full bar samples, some of them slivered down to 1/64th notes which sounds like someone channel surfing annoyingly fast or constantly cranking the radio dial in their car.",
     "zh": "没过多久，这就给了制作人一个想法：用切片采样而不是整小节采样来给作品「加毛刺」。有些切片细到 1/64 音符，听起来像有人疯狂快速地换台，或者在车里不停地拧收音机旋钮。"
    },
    {
     "en": "The microscopic slicing of samples is where the genre gets its name, first coined by Philip Sherburne in a 2001 article that no one has ever read because why would anyone want to read about music like this? It also doesn't hurt that the music is sparse and isolated, with tight and punchy percussion and light on pads and leads. Only one place on Earth would want to make such music: Detroit.",
     "zh": "采样的微观切片正是这个流派名字的由来，最早由 Philip Sherburne 在 2001 年的一篇文章里提出——那篇文章从没人读过，因为谁会想读关于这种音乐的文章呢？这音乐本身稀疏孤立，打击乐紧实有力，pad 和主音都很轻，这也没什么坏处。地球上只有一个地方会想做这种音乐：底特律。"
    },
    {
     "en": "Microhouse arrived at the perfect time, when IDM was blowing up and people were finally started to warm up to the clicks and cuts oddity of the experimental Glitch crowd. All you need to do is attach a steady beat and a rhythmic cadence to those random and arhythmic slices and they would totally work as a dance genre.",
     "zh": "Microhouse 来得正是时候——那时 IDM 正在爆发，人们终于开始接受实验性 Glitch 圈子那种咔嗒咔嗒的怪东西。你只需要给那些随机、无节奏的切片接上一个稳定的节拍和节奏韵律，它们就完全能当舞曲用。"
    },
    {
     "en": "Chalk it up to Detroit for giving us another bare-bones genre to stroke our chins to, but the genre left the confines of the American Midwest soon afterward and found a captive audience in Detroit's sister culture: Germany. There it evolved into the pinnacle of dancefloor zombification: Minimal Tech.",
     "zh": "归功于底特律又给了我们一个让人托着下巴沉思的骨感流派，但没过多久它就离开了美国中西部，在底特律的姊妹文化——德国——找到了死忠听众。在那里它进化成了舞池僵尸化的顶峰：Minimal Tech。"
    },
    {
     "en": "Past this point, nothing remains except the plinky plonk.",
     "zh": "过了这一点，剩下的就只有叮叮咚咚了。"
    },
    {
     "en": "Ish.",
     "zh": "Ish."
    }
   ],
